redesign.proposal:
```sql
CREATE TABLE mcp_request (
  account_id  bigint NOT NULL REFERENCES account(id),
  request_id  text   NOT NULL,
  input_hash  text   NOT NULL,
  turn_id     bigint NOT NULL REFERENCES turn(id),
  result      jsonb  NOT NULL,
  created_at  timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
  PRIMARY KEY (account_id, request_id));
```
`writeAsMcp(r)`:
1. lock the account row;
2. `SELECT input_hash, result FROM mcp_request WHERE account_id AND request_id`, and replay or refuse if found;
3. `INSERT turn (channel='mcp', calls=[call], action, record='')`;
4. `applyCall(tx, {accountId, batchId: turn.id})`;
5. `UPDATE turn SET record`;
6. `INSERT mcp_request`.
A refusal or no-op still throws to roll back, as today. The account lock makes the read-then-insert race-free, and the PK is the backstop. With no request_id, skip mcp_request. Delete the fake-row insert, settleBatchRow, `WriteRequest.waId`, and the wa_id compare in replay. Pruning after about 24 h can come later; it is not needed at this scale. Migration: `INSERT INTO mcp_request SELECT a.id, substr(i.wamid,5), i.payload->>'input_hash', i.id, i.payload->'result', i.received_at FROM inbound_message i JOIN account a ON a.wa_id=i.wa_id WHERE i.channel='mcp' AND i.payload ? 'result'`.

src/history.ts replaces src/undo.ts, src/undo-facts.ts, src/undo-reminders.ts and the undo halves of ledger, facts and reminders/db.

interface Store { name: 'movement'|'fact'|'reminder'; table: string; image(sql): Fragment;
  /** A domain reason a row that matches still cannot be taken back. */ blocked?(tx, accountId, id, to: Image|null, now): Promise<boolean>;
  /** Columns outside the image to set when an image is written back. */ onRestore?(image, now): Fragment }
openBatch(tx, {accountId, turnId, undoes?}): Promise<Batch>
create(tx, batch, store, values) / change(tx, batch, store, id, set): Promise<{id, before, after} | null>
undoLast(tx, {accountId, turnId, askedAt, now, stores}): Promise<UndoOutcome>
UndoOutcome = undone {batch, items: {store, id, before, after}[]} | nothing | expired | stale {items} | refused {store}

Algorithm: (1) newest batch not undone; none -> nothing; `askedAt - created_at > 24 h` -> expired, both on the database clock. (2) Fold its effects per (store, entity): before = first.before, after = last.after; drop an entity that is absent on both sides or unchanged. This one fold is the 'net of nothing' rule and the 'updated then forgot in one message' case. If nothing is left, mark the batch undone and look at the one before, so the stack cannot jam. (3) For each entity, read the row FOR UPDATE and require its image to equal `after` (jsonb equality in SQL, both sides built by the same expression) and `blocked` to be false; any failure -> stale, nothing written. (4) Open the undo batch (`undoes`), write each `before` back in reverse order (a created row gets `removed_at`), one effect each.

The second 'deshacer' needs no chain walk: undoing B2 writes B1's image back, so the row equals B1's `after` again. Reminder hook: `blocked` when a one-off was sent (`next_due_at IS NULL` and not removed), when a cancelled one has no occurrence after `now`, or when the cap would be passed; `onRestore` recomputes `next_due_at`. Fact hook: `blocked` when another live row holds the key. Movement: none. The MCP rule becomes the caller's argument (`stores: ['movement','fact']` -> `refused`), not a channel read in the engine. With commit-ordered batches and one account lock, stale is unreachable for movements and facts; it stays as a cheap check and is real for reminders, where the sweep writes too.
